    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/420765617/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """who carried in his things was frightened by the house dog who barked. “Suo cane”, he said, “ha una  voce di poeta ”, he said. We have a marvellous cook. After an excellent dinner, we read poetry and I worked on the review of Bernhard’s Lotto which the editor of the Gazette des Beaux Arts has asked me for.
Wednesday. Jan. 23.  ‘95 
Worked on the Lotto. After lunch B and I went to town and desolated ourselves trying to think what to do with his room. Then I called on Emily, and she suggested  sacking  for his walls, to which I added a dad of matting, green and buff. I called at the Palmerino and had some talk with Vernon and Miss Thompson about Mr. Hamilton, whom also I saw. English poetry in the evening. """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 91 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1894-95> .

